/**
 * # Built artifacts and resources
 *
 * Artifacts are intentionally produced or modified material bodies. Resource names
 * their role in activities or plans directed toward goals. This namespace
 * distinguishes the artifact from its use as a resource and places both within
 * the universal BuiltEnvironment domain.
 */
ontology infrastructure
	version 1.0
	using imod, physical, earth
	in domain imod:BuiltEnvironment;
	
/**
 * ## Artifacts as material bodies
 *
 * Artifact records intentional production or modification of a material body.
 * The distinction is useful in ordinary descriptions of built things, while
 * leaving the body's unity and boundaries to its more specific kinds.
 * Artifact is an abstract organizing category; models target suitable concrete
 * specializations.
 *
 * Being an artifact does not establish current usefulness, successful
 * functioning or a Resource role. The producer need not be human, and the
 * meaning is not restricted to Earth. Informational artifacts and constructed
 * cavities are outside this material-body class; a cavity remains distinct
 * from the material structure that bounds it.
 */
abstract thing Artifact
    "An intentionally produced or modified material body."
    is physical:MaterialBody
    ;

/**
 * ## Artifact production and modification
 *
 * These activities describe the provenance of observed artifacts.
 * `infrastructure:ArtifactProduction` brings a new artifact entity into
 * existence; `infrastructure:ArtifactModification` changes a continuing material
 * body. Modification may turn a natural body into an artifact without creating
 * a new body. A repair may modify an existing artifact while producing a new
 * component; the distinction is relative to each identified entity.
 *
 * Models establish actual histories and identity continuity under the relevant
 * individuation criteria. They identify participating agents, material inputs,
 * tools and outcomes where those are known and relevant. Participation alone
 * does not establish material incorporation or derivation. Observing an artifact
 * does not require a complete production history; unknown history remains unknown.
 * Reuse need not involve either production or physical modification.
 *
 * An artifact's kind, its product classification and the classification of an
 * economic activity are separate descriptions. Appropriate authorities may
 * supply established classifications within their respective scopes.
 */
abstract process ArtifactProduction
    "An activity bringing a new artifact entity into existence, according to the model's individuation criteria."
    is physical:Activity;

abstract process ArtifactModification
    "An activity intentionally changing an existing material body while its identity continues, yielding or altering an artifact."
    is physical:Activity;

abstract role Resource
     "A resource is the role that objects plays in the context of activities or plans to achieve goals";

/**
 * ## Experimental first-level articulation
 *
 * These observations extend the shared vocabulary for multidisciplinary review.
 * Their declaration-local review records preserve rationale, alternatives and
 * exact validation evidence. Experimental inclusion is distinct from recorded
 * community acceptance; models still establish occurrence, support and outcomes.
 */

@review(
    schema="klab.review/1",
    proposal="wv-review-20261023-0068",
    revision="strawman-integration-r1",
    maturity="experimental",
    controversy="unassessed",
    technical_validation="not_recorded",
    community_acceptance="not_recorded",
    review_record="../docs/reviews/domain-integration-20261023/records/earth/infrastructure.json"
)
thing Building
    "An individually bounded constructed building material body, distinct from its occupants or organization."
    is infrastructure:Artifact;

@review(
    schema="klab.review/1",
    proposal="wv-review-20261023-0069",
    revision="strawman-integration-r1",
    maturity="experimental",
